{"number": 243, "text": "            Appendix          4\n    Short              forms      (he’s    / I'd   / don't,      etc.)\n                  | In spoken English we usually pronounce “I am’ as one word. The short form (I’m) is A way of\n\n                    writing this:\n                           lam    —     I'm           ©)      feeling tired this morning.\n                            itis  —     it’s          OQ  “Do you  like this jacket?” “Yes, it’s nice.’\n                      theyhave                                    sold their car.\n                                                etc.\n\n                     When   we write short forms, we use’ (an apostrophe):\n                                  I’m            he’s   you       > you’ve             —\n\n\n            (42      We  use these forms with I/he/she, etc.:\n                      am       —>   ‘m    Im\n                      is            's                  she’s   it’s\n                      are      —    ‘re                               were     you're\n                      have     —    ‘ve   I’ve                        we've    you've\n                      has           's           he’s   she’s    it’s\n                      had      —>   ‘d                  she'd         we'd     you'd  _\n                      will     —>   ‘tl   it     he'll  she'll        we'll    you'll\n                       would        ‘d                  she'd          wed     youd      they'd\n\n                          ©)  I’ve got some new shoes.\n                           ©) We'll probably go out tonight.\n                              It’s 10:00. You’re late again.\n                      ’s = is or has:\n                           O  She's going out tonight. (she’s going = she is going)\n                              She’s gone out. (she’s gone = she has gone)\n                      ‘d = would or had:\n                              a: What  would you  like to eat?\n                                 I'd like a salad, please. (I’d like = | would like)\n                            >) | told the police that I’d lost my passport. (I'd lost = | had lost)\n                      Do  not use         etc. at the end of a sentence (see Unit 38):\n                           >  “Are you tired?”             (not Yes, I'm.)\n                           +) She isn't tired, but he is. (not he's)\n\n\n                4.3   We  use short forms with I/you/he/she, etc, but you can use short forms (especially ’s) with other\n\n                           ©  who’s  your favorite singer? (= who is)\n                           —) What’s  the time? (= what  is)\n                           ©) There’s a big tree in the yard. (= there is)\n                            ©) My sister’s working in London. (= my sister is working)\n                           ©        gone  out. (= Eric has gone out)\n                            5  What  color’s your car? (= What color is your car?)\n\n\n           232", "char_count": 2913, "mean_conf": 78.91, "word_count": 354, "words": [{"text": "writing", "x0": 407, "y0": 374, "x1": 542, "y1": 419, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "this:", "x0": 556, "y0": 371, "x1": 630, "y1": 407, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "lam", "x0": 551, "y0": 456, "x1": 637, "y1": 490, "conf": 89, "bold": true}, {"text": "—", "x0": 696, "y0": 458, "x1": 744, "y1": 484, "conf": 86, "bold": false}, {"text": "I'm", "x0": 800, "y0": 451, "x1": 862, "y1": 488, "conf": 76, "bold": true}, {"text": "©)", "x0": 1094, "y0": 439, "x1": 1132, "y1": 479, "conf": 86, "bold": false}, {"text": "I’m", "x0": 1172, "y0": 445, "x1": 1234, "y1": 481, "conf": 23, "bold": true}, {"text": "feeling", "x0": 1250, "y0": 443, "x1": 1374, "y1": 490, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "tired", "x0": 1388, "y0": 441, "x1": 1475, "y1": 478, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "this", "x0": 1491, "y0": 440, "x1": 1557, "y1": 476, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "morning.", "x0": 1573, "y0": 439, 